1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,367
Hi — this is Bella, for Chaitanya.

2
00:00:02,433 --> 00:00:06,893
This video is about how the AI tutor
actually knows the textbook — through

3
00:00:06,933 --> 00:00:08,400
lookup, not training.

4
00:00:08,867 --> 00:00:12,827
Almost everyone guesses wrong about
this, and the guess changes how you

5
00:00:12,867 --> 00:00:14,000
judge the whole system.

6
00:00:14,230 --> 00:00:18,557
When people hear there's an AI tutor
inside a textbook, they assume we fed

7
00:00:18,597 --> 00:00:20,463
the book to the AI and it learned it.

8
00:00:20,963 --> 00:00:22,063
That is not what happens.

9
00:00:22,697 --> 00:00:24,363
Nothing is trained on the textbook.

10
00:00:24,897 --> 00:00:28,457
There is no training run,
and no model of our own anywhere in this

11
00:00:28,497 --> 00:00:29,197
project.

12
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:34,110
The model we use is OpenAI's,
off the shelf — the same one anybody can

13
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:34,850
rent.

14
00:00:34,917 --> 00:00:35,850
It never changes.

15
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:38,383
It doesn't know our books,
and it never will.

16
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:41,810
So how does it answer a
question about chapter twelve?

17
00:00:41,850 --> 00:00:42,583
It looks it up.

18
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:44,150
Here's the real sequence.

19
00:00:44,683 --> 00:00:46,183
A student asks a question.

20
00:00:46,783 --> 00:00:50,743
Before that question reaches the model
that writes the answer, the system

21
00:00:50,783 --> 00:00:54,943
searches the textbook and pulls out
the handful of passages most relevant

22
00:00:54,983 --> 00:00:55,917
to what was asked.

23
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:57,913
Then it sends both things together:

24
00:00:58,347 --> 00:01:00,247
the question, and those passages.

25
00:01:00,913 --> 00:01:02,847
So the model isn't remembering the book.

26
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:07,140
It's handed the relevant pages at
the moment it's asked, and it answers

27
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:08,447
from what's in front of it.

28
00:01:08,947 --> 00:01:11,647
It's an open-book exam,
not a study session.

29
00:01:11,890 --> 00:01:13,257
This sounds like a workaround.

30
00:01:13,923 --> 00:01:16,357
It's actually the better design,
for three reasons.

31
00:01:17,157 --> 00:01:17,857
First:

32
00:01:18,023 --> 00:01:19,057
you can fix things.

33
00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:23,223
Correct a mistake in chapter twelve
and the tutor is corrected too.

34
00:01:23,723 --> 00:01:25,890
If it had been trained,
you would have to train it again.

35
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:26,627
Second:

36
00:01:26,667 --> 00:01:27,367
it can cite.

37
00:01:27,667 --> 00:01:31,860
Because the answer came from specific
passages, the tutor can point at the

38
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:32,967
section it got them from.

39
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:34,967
A trained model can't.

40
00:01:35,267 --> 00:01:37,733
It just knows things,
and you can't check where from.

41
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:38,487
Third:

42
00:01:38,527 --> 00:01:39,693
it's dramatically cheaper.

43
00:01:40,393 --> 00:01:42,727
Renting a model per
question costs about a cent.

44
00:01:43,493 --> 00:01:48,120
Running our own would need a GPU server
on all day — hundreds of dollars a

45
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:50,860
month, before anyone
asked a single question.

46
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:52,567
There is one catch worth knowing.

47
00:01:53,233 --> 00:01:57,027
That search only works because the
textbook was indexed ahead of time —

48
00:01:57,067 --> 00:01:59,233
catalogued, so it can
be searched quickly.

49
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:03,333
By default, that index is rebuilt
whenever the book is built.

50
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:07,447
But if the content changes and the
index doesn't get rebuilt, the tutor

51
00:02:07,487 --> 00:02:09,387
keeps answering from the old version.

52
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:11,020
Confidently.

53
00:02:11,253 --> 00:02:12,987
With no error and no warning.

54
00:02:13,487 --> 00:02:17,387
So if a tutor ever seems to be
describing a chapter that no longer exists

55
00:02:17,653 --> 00:02:19,253
— that's the first thing to check.

56
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:20,443
So, the short version:

57
00:02:20,843 --> 00:02:21,843
nothing is trained.

58
00:02:22,477 --> 00:02:26,437
The tutor searches the book, takes
the relevant pages, and answers from

59
00:02:26,477 --> 00:02:27,177
those.

60
00:02:27,610 --> 00:02:31,603
Which means it stays correct as the
book changes, it can show you where it

61
00:02:31,643 --> 00:02:34,410
got something, and it costs
cents instead of hundreds.

62
00:02:34,977 --> 00:02:37,177
That's the trade we made,
and it's the right one.

63
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:38,650
Let's recap with Claude.

64
00:02:39,117 --> 00:02:40,683
Nothing is trained on the textbook.

65
00:02:41,383 --> 00:02:44,943
The system searches the book and
hands the model the relevant passages

66
00:02:44,983 --> 00:02:46,150
along with the question.

67
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:51,343
That's why a fix to the content fixes
the tutor, why it can cite, and why

68
00:02:51,383 --> 00:02:52,083
it costs cents.

69
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:53,240
Your turn.

70
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:54,673
Take this into Claude:

71
00:02:55,107 --> 00:02:58,833
My product answers questions about a
document set by looking passages up

72
00:02:58,873 --> 00:03:01,173
at question time instead
of training on them.

73
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:05,600
Walk me through what that buys me
and what it costs — especially how I

74
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:09,333
stop the search index drifting out
of step with the content, and how I'd

75
00:03:09,373 --> 00:03:13,000
tell whether an answer came from my
documents or from the model's own

76
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:13,907
prior knowledge.

77
00:03:14,473 --> 00:03:16,907
That second half is the
one worth pushing on.

78
00:03:17,107 --> 00:03:20,800
A lookup system that quietly falls
back on what the model already knew

79
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:22,873
looks exactly like one that's working.

80
00:03:23,663 --> 00:03:24,363
Lookup, Not Training.

81
00:03:24,730 --> 00:03:26,797
For Chaitanya and Humanitarians AI.
